<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carpathian Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Carpathians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Carpathian Mountains form an arc about 1,500 km long across central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-gates">Iron Gates</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. They enclose the Pannonian Basin, which the Hungarians entered across their passes in 895. The range has long been a frontier between peoples and states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains.jpg" alt="Carpathian Mountains" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jakub Krajňák · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morskie_Oko_in_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Carpathians form an arc across Central and Southeast Europe, about 1,500 kilometres long and covering 190,000 square kilometres. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> they are the most extensive mountain system in Europe. They do not form an uninterrupted chain but consist of several geologically distinct groups, which run from the Western Carpathians through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-carpathians">Eastern Carpathians</a> to the Southern Carpathians. Their width varies from 12 to 500 kilometres. The highest peak, Gerlachovský štít in the Tatra Mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, rises 2,655 metres. About half the range lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, 21 per cent in Slovakia and 10 per cent each in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>.</p><p>The mountains are separated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and are surrounded on all sides by plains, among them the Pannonian plain to the south-west. They form the watershed between the northern seas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. They arose in the Alpine orogeny, in the Mesozoic and Cenozoic eras, and iron, gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> were found in great quantities in the Western Carpathians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Carpates is first recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Geographia</a> of Ptolemy in the second century CE, though in most languages the modern form of the name is a neologism. It is strongly associated with the Dacian tribes called Carpes or Carpi, who lived east of the mountains. Late Roman documents called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-carpathians">Eastern Carpathians</a> the Montes Sarmatici, the Sarmatian Mountains. Hungarian documents of the thirteenth to fifteenth centuries called them Thorchal or Tarczal, or less often Montes Nivium, the Snowy Mountains, and the Rus' chronicles called them the Hungarian Mountains.</p><p>The origin of the name is not firmly established. Some connect it with a root meaning rock or rugged, others with one meaning to turn, referring to the bend of the range. After the Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>, he brought back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> over 165 tons of gold and 330 tons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The mountains stretch through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. They are habitat for the largest European populations of brown bears, wolves, chamois and lynxes, and they hold over a third of all European plant species. Romania has one third of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s thermal and mineral waters, many of them in the Carpathians. The area is the most seismically active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, and the last volcanic activity occurred at Ciomadul about 30,000 years ago.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carpathian Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 568 the Lombards left Pannonia for Italy, and the Avars took over the whole basin, the great plain enclosed by the <b>Carpathians</b> and the Alps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Wallachia lies north of the Lower Danube and south of the Southern <b>Carpathians</b>, divided by the Olt River into Muntenia in the east and Oltenia in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Through colonisation and acculturation the formative Pre-Cucuteni/Trypillia A culture took shape, and during the 5th millennium BCE it spread from its homeland between the Prut and the Siret along the eastern foothills of the <b>Carpathians</b> to the plains of the Dnieper and Southern Bug.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cucuteni-trypillia-culture">Cucuteni–Trypillia culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carpi lived in Moldavia, east of the <b>Carpathians</b> and outside Roman Dacia, from the 2nd century CE.</blockquote><a href="/state/carpi">Carpi</a></li><li><blockquote>In 895 Pechenegs and Bulgarians attacked Etelköz, and the Hungarians crossed the <b>Carpathians</b> into the basin, founding the Principality of Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-tribal-federation">Hungarian tribal federation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Pecheneg attacks drove them from Etelköz, the Hungarian tribal federation under Árpád crossed the <b>Carpathians</b> in 895.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hungary">Principality of Hungary</a></li></ul></section>
